# Migrations

`howdy_auth` creates and upgrades its own tables. You decide when: nothing
is migrated behind your back as the app starts.

The runner, `howdy/migration`, comes from
[Howdy Database](/docs/database/migrations), so add `howdy_database` to
`gleam.toml` alongside `howdy_auth`. Your app can use the same runner for
its own tables.

```gleam
import howdy/auth
import howdy/authorization
import howdy/migration

pub fn migrate(db) {
  migration.run(db, [auth.schema(), authorization.schema()])
}
```

The order of the list is the order of dependency, so `auth.schema()` comes
first. Leave out `authorization.schema()` if you do not use
[roles](/docs/auth/roles).

Running it again is safe: only migrations that have not been applied run.
The whole batch happens in one transaction, and a failure rolls all of it
back. Two processes migrating at once take turns.

In a project made with `howdy new`, call it next to your own migrations. In
production, run it as a step of the deployment, before the new version of
the app starts, and back up the database first. There are no down
migrations. Stop old instances before migrating; running two versions of
the app against one schema is not supported.

## Upgrading

New versions of `howdy_auth` add migrations; they never edit old ones. Run
`migration.run` again as part of deploying each new version, before it
starts. `auth.new` refuses to start against tables that are behind.

Recent features that need new migrations include
[provider sign-in](/docs/auth/providers) (migration 9),
[account changes](/docs/auth/account) (migration 10),
[passkeys and second factors](/docs/auth/mfa) (migration 11), and
[registering with a passkey](/docs/auth/passkeys) (migration 15). Existing
users and sessions survive each of them.

If you keep [sessions in a store of your own](/docs/auth/sessions), check
that it saves each entry's `method` and `version` unchanged.

## Packages

The runner keeps a ledger, `howdy_migrations`, of what each *package* has
applied, with a checksum of every migration. A package is a name and a list
of ordinary `gloo/migration` values:

- A package owns the tables whose names begin with its name and an
  underscore. Auth owns `howdy_auth_*`, and authorization owns
  `howdy_authz_*`.
- Versions are positive and increase within a package. Two packages can
  both have a version 1.
- A migration that has been applied is never edited. To change a schema,
  append a migration with a higher version.

This ledger is separate from the one `gloo/runner` keeps for your app's own
migrations, and the two sit happily in one database. Do not run the auth
migrations through `gloo/runner` as well.

## Tables of your own

Your app can join the same transaction with a package of its own, which is
the tidy way to create tables that reference users:

```gleam
import gloo/migration as gloo_migration

migration.run(db, [
  auth.schema(),
  migration.Package("app", [
    gloo_migration.new(
      1,
      "create_profiles",
      "CREATE TABLE app_profiles (
         user_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES howdy_auth_users(id),
         display_name TEXT NOT NULL
       )",
    ),
  ]),
])
```

Statements are separated by semicolons, so a semicolon inside a string
literal is not supported, and the SQL must not begin or commit
transactions.

When the SQL differs between databases, append
`migration.per_database(postgres:, sqlite:)` to the statements they share.
Only the matching half runs:

```gleam
gloo_migration.new(
  2,
  "add_seen",
  "CREATE TABLE app_notes (id TEXT);"
    <> migration.per_database(
      postgres: "ALTER TABLE app_notes ADD COLUMN seen TIMESTAMPTZ",
      sqlite: "ALTER TABLE app_notes ADD COLUMN seen BIGINT",
    ),
)
```

For a small value per user, a [field](/docs/auth/fields) needs no table at
all.

## Checked at startup

`auth.new` and `authorization.new` check that the recorded history matches
the installed package, and that the tables still have the shape the
migrations gave them. An app that has not been migrated, or whose auth
tables were altered by hand, does not start.

You may:

- reference `howdy_auth_users(id)` from your own tables
- add a plain, non-unique index to an auth table, with a name outside the
  package's prefix, such as `ops_events_action`

You may not add columns, unique indexes or triggers to the auth tables.

The check compares what the database reports about its own schema, and
that report can change when nothing real has: after a major PostgreSQL
upgrade, or a dump and restore. If startup says the schema changed, and you
have confirmed by hand that it did not, accept the current shape from a
deployment command:

```gleam
let assert Ok(_) = migration.rebaseline(db, auth.schema())
```

`rebaseline` refuses unless the recorded history matches the package
exactly, so it cannot hide a missing or edited migration. Never call it as
the app starts.
